WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.740
- Welcome, welcome, welcome

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.560
to another exciting
edition of Market Takes.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.630
We are looking forward out at the week.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.860
Thank you so much for being with us.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.510
I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.970
but you are here with me
and I appreciate that.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.580
I'm Dion Rabouin, reporter
at the Wall Street Journal

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.080
your host for Market Takes.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.590
And look, there is going to
be a lot happening this week.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.330
We got a huge week of data

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.163
but I'm gonna tell you

00:00:22.163 --> 00:00:24.780
what the three most important things are

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.060
that are happening this week.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.610
First things first, the
first one is inflation.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:34.610
We get two big reports on
inflation this week, CPI and PPI.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:35.790
I'll tell you what those are,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.590
what you need to look for in those.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.930
Second, we will be hearing from the IMF,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.050
the International Monetary
Fund and the World Bank

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.790
on the state of the global economy.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.500
I'll tell you what to watch and listen for

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.520
throughout the week of meetings

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.730
as well as what reports are
gonna be coming from that.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.888
Third, we will get the
first earnings reports

00:00:53.888 --> 00:00:57.720
from the largest US banks,
that's coming on Friday.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.850
That's gonna tell us a lot
about not just the economy

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.500
and the market but that's
gonna tell us a lot

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.660
about this bank run, these bank collapses

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.860
that we've been seeing,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.040
this movement of money
across the banking system.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.270
We are gonna hear from those big banks

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.760
and we are gonna learn a
lot about what's going on

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.460
with the banks and how
that looks through the eyes

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.260
of those banks and what
that could tell us.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.360
That's gonna tell us a
lot about the economy.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.150
So listen, let's get into it.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.700
First things first,
inflation out this week.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.790
We get the CPI, the Consumer Price Index.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.961
I wanna put this chart
here up on your screen

00:01:28.961 --> 00:01:32.400
that's just gonna, it shows
you what we've seen from CPI.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.490
Now, going back to March
2022, where we start this,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.380
you saw inflation had already picked up

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.330
and there was an expectation that, okay,

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:40.800
we'd hit peak inflation,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.480
but then nope, you see
it picks back up there.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.180
And then in June we hit that 9.1% number.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.410
And ever since then

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.330
we've been coming down,
coming down, coming down.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:52.170
And economists predict
that inflation for March,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:56.190
for March 2023, will be about 5.2%.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.890
Now that's down from 6% in February

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.200
and down from 6.4% in January.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.427
You see that just continual
decline of inflation on the CPI.

00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:07.980
And that just illustrates,
this chart up here,

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.980
shows you how inflation
has consistently come down

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:11.970
over the past year.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.500
It's done that month over month as well.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.100
Economists predict that we'll
see 0.3% inflation for March.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.270
That's after 0.4% in
February and 0.5% in January.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.190
Now, one thing you wanna watch out for

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.630
when we get this CPI report

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.730
is the core CPI number,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.850
that strips out volatile
food and energy prices.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.200
And that's gonna be a big number,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.960
because economists actually
expect that to rise.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.790
Now, overall inflation
you saw in that chart

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.230
had been coming back down

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.670
and economists expect that to continue.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:43.050
But core, stripping out
volatile food and energy prices,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.840
is expected to rise for the
first time since September.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:46.710
That could be big

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.080
because it could alter what
the Federal Reserve is thinking

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.940
and how they're looking at inflation.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.320
You're gonna wanna watch out for that.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.596
Also important within this
CPI report is gonna be

00:02:54.596 --> 00:02:56.910
the movement of gas prices.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.820
We have seen gas prices, the
average across the country

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.070
has risen to about 360 per gallon.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.480
And out in California, it's approaching

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.660
that $5 per gallon average
throughout the state.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.000
So oil prices start to pick back up.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.320
The decline in oil prices

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.558
has been a big part of why
inflation has declined.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:14.940
It's been a big part of
why consumer sentiment

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:15.773
has been rebounding.

00:03:15.773 --> 00:03:18.240
And now we are starting to
see those gas prices pick up.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.138
We're seeing oil on the market
at about $80 per barrel.

00:03:22.138 --> 00:03:23.880
Saudi Arabia and OPEC

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.130
announced a surprise cut in production.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.480
That could push gas prices up.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.980
You're gonna wanna watch that.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.090
Now, the other thing

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.100
you're gonna wanna
watch out for this week,

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.660
CPI comes out on Wednesday,

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:36.510
Thursday we get PPI or
the Producer Price Index.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.820
Want to throw that chart
up here on your screen

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:39.653
to give you a look.

00:03:39.653 --> 00:03:42.090
Now, PPI has really led CPI.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.700
It's been about a month ahead
and we've seen the trends.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.860
Now you see there that same kind of trend,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.750
but you see things moving higher.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:53.010
On the PPI report, June
was over 10%, around 11%.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:56.517
PPI inflation, that's what
businesses are paying producers.

00:03:56.517 --> 00:03:59.640
It's AKA, what this report is also known

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.050
as the Wholesale Price Report.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.080
So it's what businesses
are paying for labor,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.790
their inputs, all those things,

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:06.630
when they go out and buy

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.280
and then they turn
around mark the prices up

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.240
and sell them to us.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.620
So that's been a leading indicator

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.900
of what overall inflation
will be for consumers.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.020
That's expected to
decline to 4.4% in March.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.860
So again, as it led CPI going
up, it's now expected to lead.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.830
It has led CPI coming down,
that's expected to continue.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.330
So this charge just illustrates

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.370
what we've seen for business prices,

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.605
the PPI over the last year,

00:04:28.605 --> 00:04:31.320
and that trend is expected to continue.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.570
One thing that I think has
been really interesting

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.908
is, you know, while
inflation is still very high

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:38.100
compared to historical norms

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.140
or at least the norms
over the past 20 years,

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.030
it seems like a lot of folks
aren't really talking about it.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.640
Wanna put one more chart
here up on your screen

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.820
and this is a chart of Google trends,

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.350
and this is just how many
people are searching for this

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.530
compared to how many people
searched for it historically.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.360
You see that big pickup there in August,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.070
that's that giant spike, you see,

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.800
that's actually after
the big peak in inflation

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.690
that happened in June, and ever since then

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.820
it's been just kind of
consistently declining.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.140
Look, inflation is not over.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.620
Inflation is still very far
from the Fed's 2% target,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.480
it was at 6% we saw in
February, even at 5.2%.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.240
If economists are correct, we
are still a long ways from 2%.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:15.180
So that chart up there

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.000
just illustrating kind
of a an interesting theme

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:18.833
that I'm seeing

00:05:18.833 --> 00:05:20.610
which is folks not really being worried,

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.160
not looking up, not Googling inflation.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.960
That's definitely
something to watch out for.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.790
Also, we are gonna get the FOMC minutes,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.130
the minutes from the
Fed's last policy meeting.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.920
Those are out on Wednesday
as well, along with CPI.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.570
Definitely something to keep your eye on.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.720
We'll be right here, Wednesday
morning, 10:00 AM Eastern,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.240
doing market takes,
breaking down that CPI,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.120
Consumer Price Index, report on inflation.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.650
I hope you'll be with us as well.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.320
The second thing you need to
be watching out for this week

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.840
is the IMF and World Bank Spring meetings.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:49.980
Those are gonna kick off this week.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.890
We've got the World Economic
Outlook released on Tuesday.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.620
That's the IMF's big picture
look at what's going on

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.170
with the globe and of
course, the economic trends.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.730
In the last update, in January,

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.890
they said they saw a,
quote, "turning point"

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:02.790
for global growth.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.400
They raised their expectations,
improved the outlook.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.810
Now overall, global growth

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:11.810
is expected to slow from 3.4%
last year to 2.9% this year.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.350
We'll see if they revise that.

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.330
Maybe there's an even rosier outlook.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.510
But the one thing that you
worry about with a rosy outlook

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.300
is that could show a pickup for inflation.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.730
Definitely something to
keep your eye out on.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.350
Previously, the World Bank has warned

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.840
of a quote, "lost decade"
for global growth,

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.670
especially in emerging markets.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.680
Talking about how things
like the war in Ukraine,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.190
the COVID-19 pandemic, and
of course high inflation,

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:39.210
have impacted the overall theme
of how the globe can grow.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:40.290
They're saying that, you know,

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:44.253
the potential growth from
2000, 2010 had been about 3.5%.

00:06:44.253 --> 00:06:47.393
Then from 2011 to 2021
that had dropped to 2.6%.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.480
Now the expectation is that
that potential growth output

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.220
will shrink to 2.2%.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.750
When there's not as much growth,

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.160
businesses can't grow as much,

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.630
incomes can't grow as much.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.360
Folks aren't able to build
their wealth as much.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.090
And you see things just kind
of get worse for everybody.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:04.770
We had seen a long running trend

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:08.310
of continual poverty
alleviation, wealth accumulation,

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.140
poor countries have been
growing a lot richer,

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.720
and that's a bonus for
everyone around the world,

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.460
including us here in wealthy countries

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.460
like the US, Europe,
Japan, things like that.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.510
That's been slowing down,

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.250
and that's definitely
something to watch out for,

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.350
big picture when you
look around the globe.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:25.020
Also worries about inflation
potentially coming back.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.210
Definitely something
to keep your eye out on

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:31.110
for these IMF and World Bank
meetings coming up this week.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.570
That kicks off on Tuesday
with a WEO report,

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.460
as they call it, World Economic Outlook.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.230
And then Wednesday you
got the big meeting,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.930
IMF managing director,
Kristalina Georgieva

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:43.260
World Bank President, David
Malpass, look out for that.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:44.760
Finally, the third thing

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.160
that you need to be watching
out for, bank earnings.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.510
We get those starting on Friday.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:52.470
The big names, JPMorgan Chase,
Wells Fargo, Citi, BlackRock

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.980
PNC Bank, BNY Mellon, the
trillion dollar teams.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.830
They are all gonna be out
with their reports this week.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.300
Not all of 'em have a
trillion dollars in assets.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.790
Just a couple of them, just
so I don't get any emails,

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.094
'cause I know y'all like to talk.

00:08:04.094 --> 00:08:06.000
But those big banks will be out

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.680
with their internet reports on Friday.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:08.700
This is gonna tell us a lot

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.850
about this worrisome banking
crisis, banking collapse,

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.470
bank run, whatever you want to call it.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.990
How much of that money
flowed into these big banks

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.030
from some of the regional
and smaller banks?

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.250
We hopefully will get
some information on that.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.240
And what does this tell us

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.070
about the state of the banking sector?

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.800
Are these banks doing something
useful with that capital?

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.710
Are they saying, okay, we're
moving into more risky assets,

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.050
that might be something
worrisome to watch out for,

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.640
you know, and this is
overall gonna tell us

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.620
how were they feeling about this deposits.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.270
Remember I told you guys a while back

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:37.590
banks don't want your money.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.240
The big banks, they have enough money,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:41.520
they really don't even know
what to do with this money.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.480
Crazy problem to have.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.250
But it is one that the
big banks are having.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.180
They got too much money.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:46.350
Now, they got even more.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.610
What are they doing with it?

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:49.680
We're also gonna talk about
how much they accessed,

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.830
how much the big banks accessed,
the Fed's discount window.

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.870
Did they borrow a lot
of money from the Fed?

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.880
We've seen $400 billion in increase

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:57.810
in the Fed's balance sheet,

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.400
how much of that went to big banks?

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.430
Finally, we are gonna see
their loan loss provisions.

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.900
How much of the big banks worried

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.520
about losing money on loans?

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.380
Are they putting a lot
of money to the side

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.180
and saying, okay, we think a lot of people

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:10.980
aren't gonna be able to
pay back their loans.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.390
That's something to look out for.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:15.480
Hey listen, that's gonna do it
for us here on Market Takes.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.460
I appreciate you guys so
much for being with us.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.920
I am Dion Rabouin, reporter
at the Wall Street Journal.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.300
I know you could have been
anywhere in the world,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.290
but you're here with me.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:23.340
I thank you for that.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.200
We hope you'll be with us on Wednesday

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.753
when we do it again next
time on Market Takes.

